We start by choosing 2 dice out of 6 to show 1s. This can be done in $\binom{6}{2}$ ways. Then, we choose 2 dice out of the remaining 4 to show 2s. This can be done in $\binom{4}{2}$ ways. The remaining 2 dice can show any of the other 4 numbers (3, 4, 5, 6), which gives us $4^2$ possibilities for these dice.

Therefore, the number of favorable outcomes is $\binom{6}{2} \times \binom{4}{2} \times 4^2$.

