Alright, so I need to solve this equation: (3x + 2)/(x - 1) = 4. Let me think about how to approach this. 

First, maybe I should get rid of the denominator to make it easier to handle. To do that, I can multiply both sides of the equation by (x - 1). That should eliminate the denominator on the left side. 

So, multiplying both sides by (x - 1) gives me: 3x + 2 = 4(x - 1). 

