The product of two even functions $ f(x) $ and $ g(x) $ is analyzed as follows:

An even function satisfies the condition $ f(-x) = f(x) $ for all $ x $. Similarly, $ g(-x) = g(x) $. The product function $ h(x) = f(x)g(x) $ is studied by examining $ h(-x) $:

$$
h(-x) = f(-x)g(-x)
$$

Since both $ f $ and $ g $ are even functions, we substitute the even function property:

$$
f(-x) = f(x), \quad g(-x) = g(x)
$$

Thus,

$$
h(-x) = f(x)g(x) = h(x)
$$

This shows that $ h(-x) = h(x) $, which is the defining property of an even function. Therefore, the product of two even functions is even.

$$
\boxed{even}
$$

