So, since both f and g are even, I can write that f(-x) = f(x) and g(-x) = g(x). Now, I need to check what happens when I take the product f(x)g(x) and plug in -x. 

Let me compute [f(x)g(x)] at -x. That would be f(-x) * g(-x). But since both f and g are even, substituting -x gives me f(x) * g(x). So, [f(-x)g(-x)] = f(x)g(x). 

Wait, that means that the product f(x)g(x) satisfies the condition for being even. Because when you replace x with -x, the value doesn't change. So, the product is even. 

Is there a case where this might not hold? Let me think. Suppose f(x) and g(x) are both even. Then their product should also be even. For example, if f(x) = x² and g(x) = x², then their product is x⁴, which is even. If f(x) = cos(x) and g(x) = cos(x), then the product is cos²(x), which is also even. 

Alternatively, if one of them was odd, then the product would be odd. But here both are even, so the product is even. 

