Given this transformation, we can determine the matrix as follows:
- The first column of the matrix corresponds to the image of the vector $(1, 0)$ under the projection. Projecting $(1, 0)$ onto the $y$-axis gives $(0, 0)$. Therefore, the first column is $(0, 0)$.
- The second column of the matrix corresponds to the image of the vector $(0, 1)$ under the projection. Projecting $(0, 1)$ onto the $y$-axis gives $(0, 1)$. Therefore, the second column is $(0, 1)$.

Putting these columns together, we get the projection matrix:
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 \\
\end{pmatrix}
$$

